---
/**
 * hospitality / GalleryBK — "Villa Marindu"'s photo bento: a brass eyebrow, one
 * display headline, and the design's five-slot mosaic — a tall lead print
 * spanning both rows beside four smaller frames.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * the GALLERY element of "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html":
 *
 *   section padding    96px top, 0 bottom — the design gives EVERY section a
 *                      bare bottom and lets the NEXT one own the gap it wants
 *                      (HOW-IT-WORKS margin-top:100px, TESTIMONIALS
 *                      padding-top:100px). So a zero bottom here is the design,
 *                      not an omission, and it must stay zero: if this section
 *                      grew a bottom padding the gap would double wherever the
 *                      following section is a padding-top one.
 *   eyebrow            10px / .44em caps in brass                 → .kick
 *   headline           Prata 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px), lh 1.1,
 *                      margin .26em 0 36px, --bone-hi, NO max-width
 *   grid               minmax(0,2fr) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr)
 *   rows               190px 190px          gap 13px
 *   tile               --well ground, 3px radius (--r-lg), clipped
 *   lead tile          grid-row: span 2
 *
 * Unlike the sister template's mosaic, this design gives EACH tile its own 3px
 * radius rather than clipping the block as one slab — so the frames carry the
 * radius and there is no wrapping clip. That is a real difference in the two
 * designs, not a port artefact.
 *
 * Every colour is a wrapper token. No literal hex appears in this file; the
 * design's #1A211E is --well, #F4F1EA is --bone-hi, #B99552 is --accent.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (each one is DECLARED in genericContentSchema.ts) ─────────
 *   gallery.tag                          → the brass eyebrow
 *   gallery.headline                     → the display h2 (set:html)
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.image     → data-image-field, the bento tiles
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.caption   → the optional brass caption pill
 * Nothing else. This section renders no link, no button and no numeral, so it
 * declares no data-href-field and needs no anchor-remap helper — the onPage()
 * helper the sibling sections carry would have nothing to remap here.
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * Not one visible word. The design's slot placeholders ("Pool at dusk",
 * "Master bedroom", "Kitchen", "Bathroom", "Sea view") are the DESIGNER filling
 * their own template — they are captions for photographs nobody has looked at,
 * so they are not written here and no caption default is invented. An
 * uncaptioned photo is simply a photo. Likewise the design's headline is not
 * hard-coded: an owner who typed no headline gets no headline element at all,
 * because every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag.
 *
 * ── THE SECTION GATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Renders only when at least one row carries an IMAGE. It deliberately does not
 * count the tag or the headline: lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets
 * gallery.tag and gallery.headline on every site, so a gate that counted them
 * would ship an eyebrow and a headline over an empty grid. The tag and headline
 * still render (guarded) once photos exist — refusing to render them would
 * leave two live sidebar inputs wired to nothing.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, not authored ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * ROW INDEXES. Rows without an image are dropped, and the ORIGINAL array index
 * travels with the survivor as `.i`. The editor writes the data-field path
 * verbatim into the draft, so a re-indexed path would patch a different row —
 * deleting row 2's photo would send row 3's edits into row 2.
 *
 * THE COUNT LADDER. The design hard-codes five slots; content does not — an
 * owner may upload one photo or eleven. So the design's five-up is the top of a
 * ladder of layouts, each drawn for its own count so no tier can open a hole:
 *   1  one full-width print, still two rows tall
 *   2  two full-height columns on the design's lopsided 2fr / 1fr
 *   3  the tall lead plus two stacked beside it
 *   4  the design's three tracks, with the fourth frame taking both narrow ones
 *   5  the design's own grid, exactly
 *   6+ the extras flow into implicit 190px rows, and the LAST frame stretches
 *      across whatever tracks its row left empty (.fill-*) so the mosaic never
 *      ends on a ragged half-row. The stretch is computed per breakpoint,
 *      because a three-track row and a two-track row run out at different
 *      counts; it is reset entirely at one column.
 *
 * ── DECISIONS A READER MIGHT THINK WERE OVERSIGHTS ──────────────────────────
 * · The design's 36px between the head and the mosaic is carried by the HEAD
 *   BLOCK, not by the h2's own margin-bottom. It renders identically when both
 *   strings exist — the h2 keeps the design's .26em above it and adds nothing
 *   below — but an owner who clears the headline and keeps the eyebrow still
 *   gets the 36px, instead of photos glued to the underside of the eyebrow.
 * · .h2 max-width is set to none. The wrapper caps every headline at 24ch;
 *   this design's gallery h2 is the one headline it does NOT cap (its About,
 *   Why and How headlines all carry an explicit max-width, this one does not).
 * · min-height is zeroed on the frames. The wrapper's .plate floors at 180px,
 *   which would fight the design's fixed 190px rows the moment they shrink.
 * · The brass italic inside the headline comes from the wrapper's global
 *   `.h2 em` rule, not from this file: set:html builds that <em> after scoping,
 *   so no scoped selector can reach it. Nothing here needs :global() because
 *   the wrapper already styles it.
 * · The hover lift on a photo is the only thing here the static design file
 *   could not express. The wrapper ships `transition: transform .5s ease` on
 *   .ph, which is dead weight unless a section moves it, and the photo mosaic
 *   is where it belongs. It is a 1.04 scale, and reduced-motion cancels it.
 * · An apostrophe in an upload path would close the url('…') and blank the
 *   tile, so the path is percent-escaped for CSS only.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A tile is announced only when the owner gave it words. With a caption the
 * frame is role="img" named by that caption — the owner's own words — and the
 * caption is also the visible figcaption. With no caption the photo layer is
 * hidden from the tree rather than announced as "image"; inventing "Property
 * photo" would publish a description of something nobody described. The two
 * attributes are mutually exclusive and both conditional, so no literal ever
 * sits beside a conditional one.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const gallery = (content && content.gallery) || {};
const tag = gallery.tag || '';
const headline = gallery.headline || '';

type Tile = { image?: string; caption?: string };
// The legacy bare-array form lives at content.gallery itself — NOT the `gallery`
// alias above, which is the object form's home. Both shapes reach production.
const raw: Tile[] = Array.isArray(gallery.items)
  ? gallery.items
  : (Array.isArray(content?.gallery) ? (content.gallery as Tile[]) : []);

const tiles = raw
  .map((t, i) => ({
    image: (t && typeof t.image === 'string' ? t.image : '').trim(),
    caption: (t && typeof t.caption === 'string' ? t.caption : '').trim(),
    i,
  }))
  .filter((t) => Boolean(t.image));

const n = tiles.length;

// CSS-only escape: the value still travels raw into data-image-field.
const cssUrl = (s: string) => s.replace(/'/g, '%27');

/* Row completion, per breakpoint. Three tracks: the design's five fill rows 1–2
   exactly, so extras start a fresh three-wide row — a remainder of 1 stretches
   the last frame across all three, a remainder of 2 across two. Four photos is
   its own case (the fourth takes both narrow tracks). Two tracks: the lead spans
   both rows, so tiles 2 and 3 finish the first column pair and the extras run two
   per row — an odd remainder stretches the last frame across the pair. */
const rem3 = n > 5 ? (n - 5) % 3 : 0;
const fillDesk = n === 4 ? 2 : (rem3 === 1 ? 3 : (rem3 === 2 ? 2 : 0));
const fillMid = n >= 4 && (n - 3) % 2 === 1 ? 2 : 0;
---
{n > 0 && (
  <section class="gal" id="gallery">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline) && (
        <div class="gal-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="gallery.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 gal-h2" data-field="gallery.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}

      <div
        class:list={['bento', 'reveal', n === 1 && 'g-1', n === 2 && 'g-2', n === 3 && 'g-3']}
        data-delay="1"
      >
        {tiles.map((t, pos) => (
          <figure
            class:list={[
              'plate',
              'frame',
              pos === n - 1 && fillDesk === 3 && 'fill-d3',
              pos === n - 1 && fillDesk === 2 && 'fill-d2',
              pos === n - 1 && fillMid === 2 && 'fill-m2',
            ]}
          >
            <div
              class="ph"
              role={t.caption ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={t.caption || undefined}
              aria-hidden={t.caption ? undefined : 'true'}
              data-image-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.image`}
              style={`background-image:url('${cssUrl(t.image)}');`}
            ></div>
            {t.caption && (
              <figcaption class="cap" data-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.caption`}>{t.caption}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The design draws this section on the page ground with no band, 96px of air
     above and none below — the section that follows carries its own top margin.
     Beats the wrapper's `section` rule on class count. */
  .gal { padding: clamp(58px, 7.6vw, 96px) 0 0; }

  /* The design's 36px down to the mosaic. It sits on the head BLOCK rather than
     on the h2 so an eyebrow with no headline still clears the photos; with both
     present the two are the same 36px (the h2 adds nothing below itself). */
  .gal-head { margin: 0 0 36px; }

  /* The design's own gallery headline is the one h2 it does not cap, so the
     wrapper's 24ch is released here. .26em above the headline, per the design. */
  .gal-h2 {
    margin: .26em 0 0;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* ── THE MOSAIC ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The design's tracks verbatim: 2fr / 1fr / 1fr, two fixed 190px rows, 13px
     gaps, and the lead print spanning both rows. --row is a single knob so the
     breakpoints move the row height without restating the grid.

     minmax(0,…) on every track, never a bare fr: a background-image tile has no
     intrinsic width, but a caption pill does, and one long caption in a bare 1fr
     track would push the whole page sideways on a phone. */
  .bento {
    --row: 190px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: var(--row) var(--row);
    grid-auto-rows: var(--row);
    gap: 13px;
  }
  .bento .frame:first-child { grid-row: span 2; }

  /* Each frame is its own clipped plate at the design's 3px radius — the block
     is NOT clipped as one slab in this design. Ground, radius and clipping come
     from the wrapper's .plate; only geometry is set here. The wrapper floors
     .plate at 180px, which would fight the fixed rows, so it is released. */
  .frame {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .frame .ph {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }
  /* The one thing a static design file cannot state. See the frontmatter. */
  .frame:hover .ph { transform: scale(1.04); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .frame:hover .ph { transform: none; }
  }

  /* ── THE COUNT LADDER. Each tier restates the tracks for its own count, so no
        tier can leave a hole. Two compounds plus Astro's scope attribute, so
        they out-specify the base grid above and the collapse rules below —
        wherever a tier is already narrow enough to survive the collapse. */
  .bento.g-1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .bento.g-2,
  .bento.g-3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .bento.g-2 .frame { grid-row: span 2; }

  /* Row completion on the trailing frame (see the frontmatter note). */
  .bento .frame.fill-d3 { grid-column: span 3; }
  .bento .frame.fill-d2 { grid-column: span 2; }

  /* ── 900px · the three-track bento collapses to two. The lead keeps its
        two-row span, which is what makes 3, 4 and 5 photos all land on complete
        rows here. .g-1 is deliberately NOT restated — it already out-specifies
        the base, so a lone photo keeps the full width instead of becoming a
        half-width tile beside an empty track — and .g-2 / .g-3 keep the design's
        lopsided 2fr / 1fr for the same reason. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .bento {
      --row: clamp(140px, 20vw, 178px);
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 11px;
    }
    .bento .frame.fill-d3,
    .bento .frame.fill-d2 { grid-column: auto; }
    .bento .frame.fill-m2 { grid-column: span 2; }
    .gal-head { margin-bottom: 30px; }
  }

  /* ── 520px · one column. A 150px-wide tile is too small to read as a room, so
        the mosaic becomes a stack and every span is reset so nothing reaches for
        a track that is not there. Nothing may push the page sideways at this
        width: the tracks are minmax(0,…), the frames cannot exceed them, and a
        pasted URL in the eyebrow breaks instead of scrolling the body. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .bento {
      --row: clamp(150px, 44vw, 200px);
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 10px;
    }
    .bento.g-2,
    .bento.g-3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .bento .frame,
    .bento.g-2 .frame,
    .bento .frame:first-child { grid-row: auto; }
    .bento .frame.fill-m2 { grid-column: auto; }
    /* …except the solo photo, which stays a two-row print so it still reads as
       the lead image it is, and not a stray thumbnail. Declared last. */
    .bento.g-1 .frame { grid-row: span 2; }

    .gal-head .kick {
      max-width: 100%;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .gal-head { margin-bottom: 24px; }
  }
</style>
